// values is mostly numbers, but uses the string 'END' as a terminator sentinel
function sumUntilEnd(values) {
  let total = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < values.length; i++) {
    if (values[i] === 'END') break;   // string compared against number elements
    total += values[i];               // Add slot sees Smi... then a string shows up
  }
  return total;
}

model.en:
Keep the hot loop's data monomorphic: store only numbers in the array and signal the end with a separate length/index or a numeric sentinel, so the <code>Add</code> feedback slot only ever sees Smis and TurboFan's integer-add speculation never breaks:<br><code>// Carry the real length separately; array holds numbers only.
function sumUntilEnd(values, count) {
  let total = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i &lt; count; i++) {
    total += values[i]; // always a number -> stable feedback, no deopt
  }
  return total;
}</code><br>Mixing a string sentinel into a numeric hot path pollutes the feedback vector: the loop's <code>Add</code> and comparison slots see two types, so optimised code that assumed Smis hits the string, fails its speculation, and deopts back to the interpreter (the dispatch loop) — every pass. Removing the off-type value lets the budget tier the function up and keeps it on the fast tier. Alternatives: use a typed array (numbers only by construction) or a separate boolean/length channel for the terminator.

